0:{"rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[[["$","$L2","4.5",{"topic":{"id":"4.5","title":"Disk Analysis using DEFT","minutes":14,"summary":"Use filesystem metadata, file content, and application artifacts together to answer a scoped investigative question.","sections":[{"title":"Orient the examination","body":["Verify the working image and identify partitions, filesystem types, volume identifiers, and relevant time settings. A disk may contain several operating systems, encrypted volumes, or recovery partitions. Record which areas are accessible and which are not. The Sleuth Kit can expose filesystem structures, while a graphical interface may organize the same underlying information differently.","Define search terms, date ranges, and artifact categories from the investigative question. Broad keyword searches can produce irrelevant private material and false matches. Document the terms and explain why they were selected. Preserve significant results with their source paths and image offsets or equivalent identifiers."]},{"title":"Files, metadata, and relationships","body":["Examine allocated files, deleted entries where recoverable, and relevant application databases. Compare filesystem timestamps with timestamps embedded in documents or messages. A copied file may retain an older embedded creation time while receiving a new filesystem time. Neither value should automatically be treated as the true human authoring time.","Link artifacts through stable identifiers and context: a browser download record, a file hash, a shortcut, and an application log may support a sequence. Filename similarity alone is weak. Where a tool decodes a database, record the version and verify important records against the original data or a second method."]},{"title":"Interpret and report the result","body":["Separate what the filesystem records from what the examiner infers. A deleted directory entry does not identify who deleted it, and a file in a user directory does not prove that the user knowingly placed it there. Consider synchronization, temporary files, automated downloads, and shared access.","Export relevant findings into controlled presentation copies with hashes and source references. Explain the tool settings, time normalization, and limitations. A readable timeline is useful only if each event points back to an artifact. The final report should answer the question rather than overwhelm the reader with every recovered filename."]}],"practice":{"title":"Find the invoice’s context","scenario":"A synthetic image contains an invoice, a browser download record, and a shortcut with different timestamps.","steps":["Verify the image and record the filesystem context.","Compare the three artifacts and their timestamp semantics.","Use hashes and source identifiers to link the records.","Write a cautious sequence of events with alternative explanations."],"debrief":"A coherent timeline comes from understanding artifacts, not sorting every timestamp and assuming the order proves intent."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["bsa","bnss"],"sources":["cert","nist-guide","bsa"],"labId":"hash","unit":4,"objectives":["Explain orient the examination and apply it to an investigation.","Explain files, metadata, and relationships and apply it to an investigation.","Explain interpret and report the result and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-4.5","prompt":"Which is the strongest way to link a downloaded file to a browser record?","options":["Only a similar filename.","Relevant identifiers, hashes, paths, and corroborating timestamps.","The largest file on the disk.","The examiner’s intuition."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Multiple consistent identifiers and contextual records provide a more defensible link than a name alone.","unit":4}}}]],["$L3"],"$L4"]}],"isPartial":false,"staleTime":300,"varyParams":null,"buildId":"mMiCr23A_-Cpk0hZkfT7X"}
